import Mathlib

/-!
# A315817: coordination sequence Gal.4.111.3

Source: https://oeis.org/A315817 and Brian Galebach, k-uniform tilings (k <= 6) and their
A-numbers, https://oeis.org/A250120/a250120.html (tiling #203, 4-uniform tiling 111 of 151,
standard notation [3^6; (3^2.6^2)^2; 3.6.3.6]).

Conjectures stated here (OEIS A315817, FORMULA section):
  Conjectures from Chai Wah Wu, Dec 28 2021: a(n) = 2*a(n-5) - a(n-10) for n > 10. G.f.: (x^10 + 6*x^9 + 6*x^8 + 18*x^7 + 12*x^6 + 28*x^5 + 12*x^4 + 18*x^3 + 6*x^2 + 6*x + 1)/(x^10 - 2*x^5 + 1).

The tiling. Galebach gives each vertex type as its cyclic sequence of polygons together with,
for every edge, the type of the neighbour and its rotation (a prime marks a reflection):
  Gal.4.111.1: A: 6^2.3^2; A 300; B 180; A 60; C 0
  Gal.4.111.2: B: 6^2.3^2; D 60; A 180; D 240; B 180
  Gal.4.111.3: C: 3^6; A 240; A 300; A 0; A 60; A 120; A 180
  Gal.4.111.4: D: 6.3.6.3; D 240; B 300; B 120; D 120
All edges have length 1. Rebuilding the tiling from this notation gives a periodic graph.
Its translation lattice has basis t1 = (-5/2, (5√3)/2) and t2 = (-5, 0).
The vertex (x, y, i) is the point x t1 + y t2 + p_i, where p_i and the vertex type are:
  i = 0: p_i = (-6, 2√3), type A
  i = 1: p_i = (-4, 0), type A
  i = 2: p_i = (-3, 2√3), type A
  i = 3: p_i = (-5/2, (3√3)/2), type A
  i = 4: p_i = (-3/2, (3√3)/2), type A
  i = 5: p_i = (0, 0), type A
  i = 6: p_i = (-5, 2√3), type B
  i = 7: p_i = (-4, 2√3), type B
  i = 8: p_i = (-7/2, (√3)/2), type B
  i = 9: p_i = (-3, √3), type B
  i = 10: p_i = (-1, √3), type B
  i = 11: p_i = (-1/2, (√3)/2), type B
  i = 12: p_i = (-2, 2√3), type C
  i = 13: p_i = (-5, √3), type D
  i = 14: p_i = (-9/2, (3√3)/2), type D
  i = 15: p_i = (-4, √3), type D
  i = 16: p_i = (-5/2, (√3)/2), type D
  i = 17: p_i = (-2, 0), type D
  i = 18: p_i = (-3/2, (√3)/2), type D
`adj i` lists the neighbours of (0, 0, i) as triples (dx, dy, j). The edge set is invariant
under the translations, so (x, y, i) is adjacent exactly to the (x + dx, y + dy, j).
From each vertex type this graph reproduces the 50 terms of the coordination sequence in
Galebach table, and from the base vertex (0, 0, 12) (type C) the data of A315817.

The graph table was generated by a script (written with the help of an AI agent, Claude Code)
from Galebach expanded notation, with exact coordinates in Z[ζ12].
-/

namespace OMA.S_oeis_a315817

/-- `(x, y, i)` is the vertex `i` of the fundamental cell, translated by `x t1 + y t2`. -/
abbrev Vertex := ℤ × ℤ × Fin 19

/-- The neighbours of `(0, 0, i)`, as `(dx, dy, j)`. -/
def adj : Fin 19 → List (ℤ × ℤ × Fin 19) := ![
  [(0, 0, 6), (0, 1, 4), (0, 1, 12), (1, 0, 1)],
  [(-1, 0, 0), (-1, 1, 12), (0, 0, 8), (0, 1, 5)],
  [(0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 7), (0, 0, 12), (1, 0, 5)],
  [(0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 4), (0, 0, 9), (0, 0, 12)],
  [(0, -1, 0), (0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 10), (0, 0, 12)],
  [(-1, 0, 2), (-1, 0, 12), (0, -1, 1), (0, 0, 11)],
  [(0, 0, 0), (0, 0, 7), (0, 0, 14), (1, 0, 17)],
  [(0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 6), (0, 0, 14), (1, 0, 17)],
  [(0, 0, 1), (0, 0, 9), (0, 0, 15), (0, 0, 16)],
  [(0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 15), (0, 0, 16)],
  [(0, -1, 13), (0, 0, 4), (0, 0, 11), (0, 0, 18)],
  [(0, -1, 13), (0, 0, 5), (0, 0, 10), (0, 0, 18)],
  [(0, -1, 0), (0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 4), (1, -1, 1), (1, 0, 5)],
  [(0, 0, 14), (0, 0, 15), (0, 1, 10), (0, 1, 11)],
  [(0, 0, 6), (0, 0, 7), (0, 0, 13), (0, 0, 15)],
  [(0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 9), (0, 0, 13), (0, 0, 14)],
  [(0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 9), (0, 0, 17), (0, 0, 18)],
  [(-1, 0, 6), (-1, 0, 7), (0, 0, 16), (0, 0, 18)],
  [(0, 0, 10), (0, 0, 11), (0, 0, 16), (0, 0, 17)]]

def neighbors (v : Vertex) : Finset Vertex :=
  ((adj v.2.2).map (fun d => (v.1 + d.1, v.2.1 + d.2.1, d.2.2))).toFinset

/-- The vertices at graph distance at most `n` from the base vertex `(0, 0, 12)`. -/
def ball : ℕ → Finset Vertex
  | 0 => {(0, 0, 12)}
  | n + 1 => ball n ∪ (ball n).biUnion neighbors

/-- The coordination sequence: the number of vertices at distance exactly `n`. -/
def a : ℕ → ℤ
  | 0 => 1
  | n + 1 => ((ball (n + 1)).card : ℤ) - (ball n).card

open PowerSeries in
theorem main :
    (∀ n : ℕ, 10 < n → a n = 2 * a (n - 5) - a (n - 10)) ∧
      mk a * (X^10 - 2*X^5 + 1 : PowerSeries ℤ) =
        X^10 + 6*X^9 + 6*X^8 + 18*X^7 + 12*X^6 + 28*X^5 + 12*X^4 + 18*X^3 + 6*X^2 + 6*X + 1 := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_oeis_a315817
